; The char-fold match is fallback-only: a String-parameter overload still
; wins for a bare double-quoted literal, so existing code keeps resolving to
; the String method and emission stays byte-identical. String.contains has
; BOTH a u8 and a String overload; a multi-byte literal proves the String
; overload was chosen (the char overload would hard-error on >1 byte, and a
; single "H" resolving to contains(u8) would still be correct — so the
; multi-byte cases are the discriminating ones).
; Expected: PASS

var failures 0
var s "Hello, World!"

; bare literals → contains(String) (String overload wins over contains(u8))
if s.contains("Hello") = false
    set failures: failures + 1
if s.contains("World") = false
    set failures: failures + 1
if s.contains("xyz")
    set failures: failures + 1

if failures = 0
    print "PASS\n"
else
    print "FAIL\n"
